WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.762
This is Allen Company training on electrical hazards,

00:07.762 --> 00:10.900
built around real situations you'll see at a quarry.

00:10.900 --> 00:13.425
For each one, decide what you would do before the

00:13.425 --> 00:14.650
answer comes up.

00:15.780 --> 00:18.492
Scenario one. On your way to the screen deck,

00:18.492 --> 00:21.343
you see a junction box with its cover off and wires

00:21.343 --> 00:22.117
showing.

00:23.238 --> 00:26.750
Answer: stay clear and report it. Inspection and

00:26.750 --> 00:29.350
cover plates are kept on except during work.

00:29.350 --> 00:32.125
A dangerous electrical condition is corrected before

00:32.125 --> 00:35.375
the equipment is used again, and only by a qualified

00:35.375 --> 00:36.175
person.

00:37.308 --> 00:40.858
Scenario two. A belt motor quits. A coworker says

00:40.858 --> 00:43.209
he'll just open the starter and reset it while

00:43.209 --> 00:45.133
it's hot, to save time.

00:46.266 --> 00:48.941
Answer: no. Electrical equipment and circuits are

00:48.941 --> 00:51.678
deenergized before work, and the disconnect is

00:51.678 --> 00:54.078
locked out and tagged by the person who does the

00:54.078 --> 00:57.666
work. Test before you touch. Only qualified people

00:57.666 --> 00:59.703
troubleshoot electrical equipment.

01:00.828 --> 01:03.778
Scenario three. The cable to a portable pump has

01:03.778 --> 01:06.278
been run over, and someone wrapped the damage in

01:06.278 --> 01:07.503
electrical tape.

01:08.634 --> 01:11.447
Answer: take it out of service and report it.

01:11.447 --> 01:14.359
Tape isn't a proper repair. Permanent splices have

01:14.359 --> 01:17.384
to be mechanically strong, well insulated and sealed

01:17.384 --> 01:20.634
against moisture, and made by a qualified person.

01:20.634 --> 01:23.209
Route cables where equipment won't run over them.

01:24.348 --> 01:27.686
Scenario four. A road truck pulls up to a stockpile

01:27.686 --> 01:30.423
under an overhead power line and starts to raise

01:30.423 --> 01:31.273
the bed.

01:32.394 --> 01:35.631
Answer: stop. Equipment near high-voltage lines

01:35.631 --> 01:38.356
needs the line deenergized or the clearances the

01:38.356 --> 01:42.181
standard requires. At Allen: no dumping or parking

01:42.181 --> 01:45.006
near overhead lines, no raised beds,

01:45.006 --> 01:47.231
and no tarping under them. Know where the lines

01:47.231 --> 01:50.494
are: Level 1 at Boonesboro, the surface at Dix

01:50.494 --> 01:53.919
River, and the surface and Mine 1 at Clover Bottom.

01:53.919 --> 01:57.181
If a vehicle does contact a line, stay inside unless

01:57.181 --> 01:59.919
it's on fire; if you must get out, jump clear with

01:59.919 --> 02:02.631
feet together and shuffle away. Never touch the

02:02.631 --> 02:04.869
truck and the ground at the same time.

02:06.000 --> 02:09.025
Scenario five. At a sump, the dewatering pump's

02:09.025 --> 02:11.538
power cable runs through standing water.

02:11.538 --> 02:13.988
The outer jacket is cut, and the plug's ground

02:13.988 --> 02:15.625
pin is broken off.

02:16.758 --> 02:19.708
Answer: don't touch the cable or the water.

02:19.708 --> 02:22.358
Call a qualified electrician to deenergize and

02:22.358 --> 02:24.420
lock out the pump at its disconnect,

02:24.420 --> 02:27.008
then tag it out until the cable is repaired or

02:27.008 --> 02:30.370
replaced. Never use damaged cables or plugs with

02:30.370 --> 02:33.158
missing ground pins, and keep cables out of water

02:33.158 --> 02:34.183
and traffic.

02:35.304 --> 02:38.579
Recap: keep covers on and report hazards.

02:38.579 --> 02:42.079
Deenergize, lock, tag and test before work,

02:42.079 --> 02:44.829
and only qualified people do electrical work.

02:44.829 --> 02:47.641
No tape repairs. Watch overhead lines.

02:47.641 --> 02:49.516
Damaged cables get tagged out.

02:50.634 --> 02:53.796
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

02:53.796 --> 02:55.296
override this video.
